UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 7, Lesson 5: Consumer choices', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Comparing products needs criteria decided before you look at the brighter packet. Useful criteria for a water bottle are price, purpose (does it hold a school day’s water) and durability (will the lid still seal after a month). Invented classroom products are enough. You do not need a real brand, and a brand name is not evidence of quality.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same HSIE idea to a new place or source, then compare two pieces of evidence for strength.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nCompare Pencil Pack A ($3, leads snap in the packet) and Pack B ($5, leads intact, 12 pencils). Which is the better buy if the criterion is “works for a month of homework”, and what is the price difference?\n\nFEEDBACK\nPack B. A snapped lead fails the purpose. The difference is $5 − $3 = $2. Cheapest fails when the criterion is a working pencil, not the lowest tag.\n\nWATCH FOR\nChoosing only by the biggest discount sticker. A discount on an item that does not do the job is still a poor buy.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nCreate two invented lunch boxes with price, size and one durability fact. Pick one using criteria you state first. Show any subtraction you use.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one source or idea you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Commerce","learning_intention":"Comparing products needs criteria decided before you look at the brighter packet. Useful criteria for a water bottle are price, purpose (does it hold a school day’s water) and durability (will the lid still seal after a month). Invented classroom products are enough. You 